patch.body:
The neighbourhood centres of Landlust and Bos en Lommer raised the money for this memorial themselves, and on 4 May 1957 mayor Gijs van Hall unveiled it in a small green on the Hertspieghelweg; lines by the poet Jan Campert were recited at the ceremony. In 1974 it moved some 75 metres south within the same green, to the north side of the Bos en Lommerweg.

The sculptor, Gerarda Rueter, had already shown her *Zittende vrouw* (seated woman) at the Sonsbeek sculpture exhibition of 1949: a pensive figure with her hands in her lap. The original was limestone; it weathered so badly that it was replaced by a bronze cast in 1976. In 1995 a tiled platform was added around it to make room for the commemorations held here.

Look for:
- on the left of the plinth, Campert's lines from *Het lied der achttien doden* ("I knew the task that I began…");
- on the right, a verse of the Wilhelmus;
- the foundry name, Binder-Schmidt, in the base plate.
